data.content:
The lightest option is .NET's built-in **`TimeProvider`** (available since .NET 8). No DI container needed — just accept it as a constructor parameter defaulting to the real clock:

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**In tests**, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-10)); // move time back
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest approach:**
- No custom interface to define — `TimeProvider` ships in the BCL.
- The optional parameter means existing callers don't break.
- `FakeTimeProvider` comes from the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package (test project only).

